WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.402
All right. Now that we cover the concepts

2
00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.671
and the slides, let's get our hands dirty.

3
00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:09.175
We're going to build a simple but powerful document processing agent.

4
00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:13.179
The goal here isn't to build a perfect production system.

5
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.982
It's to see how OCR, set of rules,

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.185
and LLM-based reasoning all fit together.

7
00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:23.089
Here we import all the packages of our system.

8
00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.793
PIL for loading images,

9
00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:29.396
pytesseract for OCR,

10
00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.799
langchain and tools for agent scaffolding.

11
00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.903
and OpenAI models, prompt templates,

12
00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:41.007
AgentExecutor that runs the full ReAct loop.

13
00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.543
Here is where you can turn OCR

14
00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:46.079
into an actual tool the agent can call.

15
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:51.546
This function takes an image path, uses Tesseract to extract text,

16
00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:56.189
and returns the result. The @tool decorator

17
00:00:56.289 --> 00:01:00.593
turns this into something the agent can recognize and call by name.

18
00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:04.397
During the agent loop, the LLM will literally decide,

19
00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:07.000
I need to read the document first,

20
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.005
calls OCR tool, gets the text and continues reasoning.

21
00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.075
You will see later the agent's reasoning

22
00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.677
is only as good as the inputs it receives.

23
00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:20.447
Here is the first test image,

24
00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:23.149
a nice clean digital invoice.

25
00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.252
This is the ideal case for OCR.

26
00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:29.956
Perfect lighting, crisp fonts, no handwriting.

27
00:01:30.056 --> 00:01:33.626
no shadows. This is where traditional OCR shines.

28
00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:36.029
Take a second look at this. In

29
00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:38.064
your mind, how would you extract the text

30
00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:41.367
and the total value? If you're writing a script.

31
00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:45.472
regex or regular expressions or rules.

32
00:01:45.972 --> 00:01:47.974
Let's see what happens.

33
00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:51.478
Now we run the OCR tool on the invoice.

34
00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:54.781
This output is exactly what you would get

35
00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:57.050
if you piped the image through Tesseract.

36
00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.285
raw text, no structure,

37
00:01:59.586 --> 00:02:01.688
no meaning, and no understanding.

38
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.090
And this is why everything downstream,

39
00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:07.594
regular expression, ML models, extraction

40
00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.696
is often very brittle.

41
00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:14.200
Here is the moment where classic rule-based systems start to break.

42
00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:17.937
We write a simple regex for tax and total.

43
00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:23.076
Let's see what happens. It misses the tax line entirely,

44
00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:25.178
and it grabs the wrong total.

45
00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.847
it grabs the subtotal first.

46
00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:30.650
And this isn't a bug in our regex.

47
00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:34.120
This is a fundamental flaw of rules interacting

48
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.122
with noisy OCR data.

49
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.925
Regex doesn't equal understanding.

50
00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:43.930
This is how most legacy intelligent document processing systems

51
00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.932
fail silently in production.

52
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:49.435
Let's look at how the regex is set up

53
00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:51.604
for tax and total match.

54
00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:54.674
It's looking for the words tax and total

55
00:02:54.874 --> 00:02:59.078
followed by spacing and a dollar sign and a set of numbers.

56
00:02:59.746 --> 00:03:02.015
and it's reading the OCR output text.

57
00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:04.083
If we go back to the document,

58
00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.451
you can see that the word total

59
00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:09.122
comes up multiple times as subtotal and total,

60
00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:13.126
and the tax has an at symbol instead of just

61
00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:17.397
spaces which can throw off the document.

62
00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:21.367
And if you are processing millions and billions of invoices

63
00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:25.071
per year from different vendors that can have different structures.

64
00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:30.071
You can see how using something like regex can break very quickly.

65
00:03:30.743 --> 00:03:32.845
So, what went wrong?

66
00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:35.648
Our regex search for tax failed

67
00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:40.286
because the OCR output had tax at, not just tax.

68
00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:44.924
Similarly, the regex initially matched the subtotal as the total

69
00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:47.994
because it appeared earlier and fit the pattern.

70
00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.530
before the actual total.

71
00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.433
You can clearly see the limitation of regex

72
00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:56.536
on OCR text. It is very brittle.

73
00:03:56.736 --> 00:04:01.241
It depends heavily on the exact wording and layout.

74
00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:04.043
Regex has no semantic understanding.

75
00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:08.248
It doesn't know that tax refers to a tax line.

76
00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:13.052
So the main takeaway is that OCR alone gives you raw text.

77
00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:16.055
Regex provides some set of rules,

78
00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:18.858
but neither actually understands the meaning.

79
00:04:19.125 --> 00:04:22.228
That's where LLMs and agents come in.

80
00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:25.031
Here is where we build our agent,

81
00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.133
and there are three key components.

82
00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.636
The LLM, which is the brain,

83
00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.138
and we're using gpt-5-mini here,

84
00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:35.141
so I'll go ahead and upload my API key.

85
00:04:35.441 --> 00:04:38.311
Second is OCR or eyes, which we defined earlier,

86
00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:41.314
and we'll set up the OpenAI GPT model.

87
00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:45.151
And then, lastly, all the tools that agents can use.

88
00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:47.487
It tells the agent what tool

89
00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:49.622
it has and how to use them.

90
00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.625
This is the essence of the ReAct framework.

91
00:04:53.026 --> 00:04:56.129
Think, act, observe, and think again.

92
00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:00.500
Here we're creating the agent using the ReAct framework.

93
00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:03.102
We're passing the llm, the set of tools,

94
00:05:03.403 --> 00:05:05.405
and the prompt we defined above.

95
00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:08.608
Then we need an AgentExecutor object

96
00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:12.312
to run the tool-enabled loop using LangChain.

97
00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:16.783
When we ask the agent to extract the text and total,

98
00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.485
something magical happens.

99
00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:21.954
It recognizes that it needs OCR,

100
00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:24.157
so it calls the OCR tool.

101
00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:28.461
It reads semantically and returns structured JSON.

102
00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:32.231
no regex, no rules, and no templates.

103
00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:36.636
And this is why agentic systems are replacing static

104
00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:40.340
intelligent document processing or IDP pipelines.

105
00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:45.178
As you can see, the agent returned structured JSON

106
00:05:45.778 --> 00:05:48.014
and it picked up the right tax

107
00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:50.283
and the total instead of the subtotal.

108
00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:52.719
So you didn't have to create

109
00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:54.887
any rules or templates to achieve this.

110
00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:58.024
With the comprehension capabilities of LLMs,

111
00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:01.861
the agent is able to extract the information correctly

112
00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:05.465
based on the parsed result of the OCR tool.

113
00:06:06.332 --> 00:06:09.235
Our earlier example worked well because the invoice

114
00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:12.739
had a clean layout and high text quality.

115
00:06:13.239 --> 00:06:16.042
But real-world documents aren't always so kind.

116
00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:19.045
Let's explore what happens when the input gets messier.

117
00:06:19.846 --> 00:06:23.649
Here is a table from the attention is all you need paper.

118
00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.552
Tables are notoriously difficult for OCR.

119
00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:30.423
They require spatial alignment and column reconstruction,

120
00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:33.426
something Tesseract was never designed to do.

121
00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:36.429
For this table, there are no clear lines

122
00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:39.132
that separate each columns and row.

123
00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:42.802
There are empty cells and scientific numbers,

124
00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:46.706
which can throw off conventional OCR tools like Tesseract.

125
00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:49.575
So let's give the agent a new task.

126
00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:52.979
I want it to extract the training cost and FLOPs

127
00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:55.681
for English to German translation

128
00:06:56.082 --> 00:07:00.086
for all methods from the table using the OCR tool.

129
00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:04.056
I also wanted to return the result as a list

130
00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:07.260
with model name and its training cost.

131
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.562
So we wanted to extract the value

132
00:07:09.562 --> 00:07:11.464
from this particular column here.

133
00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:15.168
First, let's get the raw OCR output.

134
00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:18.171
Then, we will use the invoke function

135
00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:21.774
with a dictionary input for the agent_executor.

136
00:07:22.475 --> 00:07:24.877
and we'll display the results side by side.

137
00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:28.581
So this is the raw output of the OCR.

138
00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:31.984
And once it's done running,

139
00:07:32.285 --> 00:07:35.688
you have the output from the agent itself.

140
00:07:37.123 --> 00:07:39.725
Here we print both the OCR output

141
00:07:39.926 --> 00:07:42.895
and the output from the LLM and the agent.

142
00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:45.198
Let's look at the OCR output.

143
00:07:45.398 --> 00:07:46.999
It is absolutely chaotic.

144
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.502
exponents become apostrophes,

145
00:07:49.502 --> 00:07:52.104
decimals have become exclamation points,

146
00:07:52.104 --> 00:07:53.806
columns are shifted.

147
00:07:54.307 --> 00:07:57.810
But the agent still attempts a best effort interpretation.

148
00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.112
And this is what LLMs do really well.

149
00:08:00.513 --> 00:08:02.315
They try to understand intent

150
00:08:02.815 --> 00:08:05.084
even when the input is degraded.

151
00:08:05.651 --> 00:08:08.354
Even if the OCR output was imperfect,

152
00:08:08.955 --> 00:08:11.023
the LLM was able to get to

153
00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:13.359
the right conclusion on some of the cases.

154
00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.863
If you look at the ByteNet and the training cost,

155
00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:18.965
you can see that it's empty.

156
00:08:19.665 --> 00:08:22.768
And even though the OCR output didn't preserve the

157
00:08:23.302 --> 00:08:26.105
table structure, it was able to understand

158
00:08:26.105 --> 00:08:28.107
that there was no value present

159
00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:29.809
for this particular model.

160
00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:31.878
If you look at the second

161
00:08:31.878 --> 00:08:33.513
row, it was supposed to be empty,

162
00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:35.515
but it picked up a different value

163
00:08:35.515 --> 00:08:37.517
from a different column incorrectly.

164
00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:40.152
If you look at the actual value itself,

165
00:08:40.253 --> 00:08:44.857
it was supposed to be 1.0 times 10 to the 20th power,

166
00:08:44.957 --> 00:08:48.060
whereas the OCR read that as 1.0

167
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.565
minus 107 degrees, which is incorrect.

168
00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:56.569
Let's walk through another example and try

169
00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:59.071
to push OCR beyond its breaking point,

170
00:08:59.305 --> 00:09:01.407
handwriting.

171
00:09:01.507 --> 00:09:04.110
So this is a document that we're analyzing.

172
00:09:04.210 --> 00:09:06.412
It's a fill in the blanks exercise for

173
00:09:06.412 --> 00:09:08.881
a student named John Smith. And you can see

174
00:09:09.315 --> 00:09:12.818
The answer is that he submitted for these sentences.

175
00:09:13.119 --> 00:09:16.489
I am an honest person. I am going to school.

176
00:09:16.789 --> 00:09:20.092
They is dancing, which we know is grammatically incorrect.

177
00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:22.528
Now, let's look at the task

178
00:09:22.528 --> 00:09:24.463
that we're asking the agent to do.

179
00:09:24.864 --> 00:09:29.335
We are asking it to process the document 'fill_in_the_blanks.jpg'

180
00:09:29.569 --> 00:09:34.569
using the OCR tool and extract the following information in JSON format.

181
00:09:35.141 --> 00:09:37.043
First, I want the student name,

182
00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:40.446
then I want the student answer to all the ten questions.

183
00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:44.650
So I will run the agent again

184
00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:49.956
and pass in the document. So let's look at the output.

185
00:09:50.056 --> 00:09:52.558
The raw OCR output, as you can see,

186
00:09:52.558 --> 00:09:55.061
was not able to capture the student name.

187
00:09:55.461 --> 00:09:58.931
And the fill in the blank answers are completely incorrect.

188
00:10:00.232 --> 00:10:02.234
Instead of am, it read Aum.

189
00:10:02.935 --> 00:10:05.438
Some numbers are recognized as letters,

190
00:10:06.138 --> 00:10:09.241
and for answer nine, instead of they is dancing,

191
00:10:09.742 --> 00:10:12.244
they picked up 1% for whatever reason.

192
00:10:13.145 --> 00:10:16.749
If you look at the LLM result, it was able to assume

193
00:10:16.749 --> 00:10:20.453
and come up with the right answers for some of the questions.

194
00:10:20.853 --> 00:10:23.756
And if you look at question nine, instead of picking up

195
00:10:23.756 --> 00:10:27.059
the actual answer by the student, which was is,

196
00:10:28.327 --> 00:10:30.563
it overcorrected and

197
00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:32.064
came up with are as the

198
00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:33.799
answer, which is not what we want.

199
00:10:34.467 --> 00:10:38.004
Now let's look at a third example, a picture of a receipt.

200
00:10:38.571 --> 00:10:41.107
Receipts as you know, are extremely messy.

201
00:10:41.741 --> 00:10:44.644
It can be low resolution with thermal printing,

202
00:10:45.344 --> 00:10:48.047
it can have misaligned text and shadows.

203
00:10:48.614 --> 00:10:50.616
Let's give the agent a new task.

204
00:10:50.916 --> 00:10:54.920
I want it to process the receipt document, the JPEG,

205
00:10:55.321 --> 00:10:58.324
and evaluate whether or not the total is correct.

206
00:10:59.592 --> 00:11:02.895
Now let's compare the OCR output to the actual receipt.

207
00:11:03.496 --> 00:11:06.999
You can see that it produces a small numerical error

208
00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:12.004
and how the agent attempts to reason through the inconsistencies.

209
00:11:12.705 --> 00:11:15.307
If you look at the first line item for the food,

210
00:11:15.908 --> 00:11:19.311
the actual value is $7.95, but the OCR

211
00:11:20.346 --> 00:11:23.349
misinterpreted that as $7.99,

212
00:11:23.649 --> 00:11:26.152
which should throw off the total calculation.

213
00:11:27.953 --> 00:11:29.622
If we look at the LLM result,

214
00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:31.857
it's reading through all the line item

215
00:11:32.124 --> 00:11:34.326
provided by the OCR tool.

216
00:11:35.027 --> 00:11:36.862
And it even picks up that some

217
00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:38.731
of them are a little bit unclear.

218
00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:41.734
and it goes through the calculation to

219
00:11:42.034 --> 00:11:45.938
get the sum of all three items listed on the receipt.

220
00:11:46.639 --> 00:11:48.941
And then it compares it to the actual total

221
00:11:49.341 --> 00:11:51.477
and comes to the conclusion that

222
00:11:51.477 --> 00:11:53.946
the total does not add up correctly.

223
00:11:54.947 --> 00:11:57.950
The reasoning is solid, but the answer is incorrect.

224
00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.453
And that is because the OCR tool

225
00:12:00.453 --> 00:12:02.655
didn't capture the numbers accurately.

226
00:12:03.055 --> 00:12:05.157
So here's what we covered during the lab.

227
00:12:05.458 --> 00:12:08.661
OCR is great at reading, but terrible at understanding.

228
00:12:09.462 --> 00:12:12.064
It performs very well on clean printed text,

229
00:12:12.064 --> 00:12:14.567
providing accurate document parsing.

230
00:12:15.468 --> 00:12:18.838
However, it struggles with complex tables and handwriting.

231
00:12:19.371 --> 00:12:21.574
it can read characters

232
00:12:21.574 --> 00:12:24.176
but doesn't truly understand structure or meaning.

233
00:12:24.877 --> 00:12:26.479
And Regex breaks easily

234
00:12:26.479 --> 00:12:29.181
when OCR output changes even slightly.

235
00:12:30.783 --> 00:12:33.486
Adding an LLM layer with an agent

236
00:12:33.486 --> 00:12:35.287
introduces understanding,

237
00:12:35.287 --> 00:12:38.090
which makes information extraction

238
00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.092
and interpretation more effective.

239
00:12:42.228 --> 00:12:44.830
Agents combine tools and reasoning,

240
00:12:45.231 --> 00:12:48.834
giving us the foundation for modern document systems.

241
00:12:49.635 --> 00:12:52.438
Real world document understanding requires

242
00:12:52.538 --> 00:12:54.540
OCR, layout detection,

243
00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:57.743
vision language models, agentic workflows,

244
00:12:57.743 --> 00:13:00.746
and grounding and validation loops.

245
00:13:01.714 --> 00:13:03.716
All right, that wraps up lesson one.

246
00:13:04.116 --> 00:13:08.120
You've now seen how basic document processing systems work.

247
00:13:08.721 --> 00:13:10.723
How OCR fits in,

248
00:13:10.723 --> 00:13:13.425
and how we can layer agentic reasoning on top

249
00:13:13.425 --> 00:13:15.928
make these systems far more intelligent.

250
00:13:16.328 --> 00:13:20.199
For our next lesson, Andrea will take us deeper into

251
00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:22.835
the foundation of all these concepts.

252
00:13:23.736 --> 00:13:27.306
Lesson two, four decades of OCR evolution,

253
00:13:27.306 --> 00:13:31.110
will explore how OCR itself has transformed over the years.

254
00:13:31.544 --> 00:13:34.046
from classic engines like Tesseract

255
00:13:34.046 --> 00:13:37.850
to modern deep learning approaches like PaddleOCR.

256
00:13:37.850 --> 00:13:39.852
And why that evolution matters

257
00:13:39.852 --> 00:13:42.054
for real-world document workflows.
